Least squares and penalised regression
Ordinary least squares uses lm.fit (Python: numpy.linalg.lstsq). Ridge, lasso and elastic net are provided by glmnet (Python: scikit-learn ElasticNet for lasso and elastic net and a closed form for ridge, with the penalty scaled as in glmnet); they differ in the penalty (squared, absolute value, and a mixture of both). The lasso sets coefficients to zero.

RPython


fit_ols &lt;- function(X, dy) {
  lm.fit(cbind(`1` = 1, X), dy)$coefficients
}

## Ridge (alpha = 0), lasso (alpha = 1) and elastic net by glmnet.
fit_glmnet &lt;- function(X, dy, alpha, lambda) {
  fit &lt;- glmnet::glmnet(X, dy, alpha = alpha, lambda = lambda)
  named(as.vector(coef(fit)), X)
}

fit_ridge &lt;- function(X, dy, lambda = 1e-3) fit_glmnet(X, dy, 0, lambda)

fit_lasso &lt;- function(X, dy, lambda = 3e-4) fit_glmnet(X, dy, 1, lambda)

fit_enet &lt;- function(X, dy, lambda = 6e-4) fit_glmnet(X, dy, 0.5, lambda)


def fit_ols(X, dy):
    return np.linalg.lstsq(np.column_stack([np.ones(len(dy)), X]), dy,
                           rcond=None)[0]


# Lasso (alpha = 1) and elastic net with the penalty of glmnet,
# lam * ((1 - alpha) / 2 * b'b / sd(dy) + alpha * |b|_1), on terms scaled to
# unit variance (glmnet scales the squared penalty by sd(dy)).
def fit_glmnet(X, dy, alpha, lam):
    sx = X.std(axis=0)
    l1, l2 = lam * alpha, lam * (1 - alpha) / dy.std()
    model = ElasticNet(alpha=l1 + l2, l1_ratio=l1 / (l1 + l2), tol=1e-12,
                       max_iter=100000, precompute=True)
    model.fit((X - X.mean(axis=0)) / sx, dy - dy.mean())
    b = model.coef_ / sx
    return np.concatenate([[dy.mean() - X.mean(axis=0) @ b], b])


# Ridge (alpha = 0) has a closed form, with the same scaling as above.
def fit_ridge(X, dy, lam=1e-3):
    sx = X.std(axis=0)
    Z = (X - X.mean(axis=0)) / sx
    A = Z.T @ Z / len(dy) + lam / dy.std() * np.eye(X.shape[1])
    b = np.linalg.solve(A, Z.T @ (dy - dy.mean()) / len(dy)) / sx
    return np.concatenate([[dy.mean() - X.mean(axis=0) @ b], b])


def fit_lasso(X, dy, lam=3e-4):
    return fit_glmnet(X, dy, 1, lam)


def fit_enet(X, dy, lam=6e-4):
    return fit_glmnet(X, dy, 0.5, lam)

Exploring a method
Each method has one hyperparameter: the threshold of STLSQ, the penalty \(\lambda\) of ridge, lasso and elastic net, or the number of terms \(k\) of subset selection. The left panel shows the scaled coefficients of g001 for the chosen setting, and the right panel the held-out trajectory error (20 genotypes) for all settings of the method.

d3 = window.d3          // local copies, see js/
Plot = window.Plot
hyper = FileAttachment("data/interactive/hyper.json").json()
viewof method = select("Method", Object.keys(hyper.methods), "STLSQ")
spec = hyper.methods[method]
viewof step = slider(spec.param, spec.values, Math.floor(spec.values.length / 2), v =&gt; (+v).toPrecision(2))












































{
  const all = Object.values(hyper.methods);
  const reach = d3.max(all.flatMap(m =&gt; m.effect.flat().map(Math.abs)));
  const rmse = [d3.min(all.flatMap(m =&gt; m.rmse)), 0.8];   // cap: very large penalties are off scale
  const rows = hyper.terms.map((term, i) =&gt; ({term, effect: spec.effect[step][i]}));
  const curve = spec.values.map((value, i) =&gt; ({value, rmse: spec.rmse[i]}));
  const scale = spec.param === "terms" ? "linear" : "log";
  const coefficients = Plot.plot({
    width: 340, height: 300, marginLeft: 50,
    x: {domain: [-reach, reach], label: "scaled coefficient"},
    y: {domain: hyper.terms, label: null},
    marks: [Plot.barX(rows, {x: "effect", y: "term",
              fill: d =&gt; d.effect &gt; 0 ? "#014E81" : "#8B1E3F"}),
            Plot.ruleX([0])]
  });
  const error = Plot.plot({
    width: 340, height: 300,
    x: {type: scale, label: spec.param, tickFormat: ".2~g"},
    y: {domain: [rmse[0] - 0.01, rmse[1] + 0.01], label: "held-out RMSE (t/ha)"},
    marks: [Plot.line(curve, {x: "value", y: "rmse", stroke: "#9CA3AF", clip: true}),
            Plot.dot(curve, {x: "value", y: "rmse", fill: "#9CA3AF", r: 3, clip: true}),
            Plot.dot([curve[step]], {x: "value", y: "rmse", fill: "#8B1E3F", r: 6, clip: true})]
  });
  return html`&lt;div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:1rem"&gt;${coefficients}${error}&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;p class="interactive-note"&gt;${spec.nterms[step]} of ${hyper.terms.length} terms retained.&lt;/p&gt;`;
}






